} return 0; } NOTES', 12, 26, 7),
  ('48922e84-2bd0-5918-89da-fc76e8196a07', '78afdd63-c3a3-5e60-90e1-90908dc10f48', '9', 'The Range And Scope Of Computers', '#### 9.0. Introduction

Computers are fast becoming our way of life and one cannot imagine life without computers in today''s world. Their importance can be attributed to the fact that almost every sphere of our life is affected. We find their applications particularly in fields where computations are required to be done at a very fast speed and where data is so complicated that the human brain finds it difficult to cope up with.

#### 9.1. Classification of Computers

Computers range from very small to very large machines with some capable of doing millions of calculations in a single second, while others may take long periods of time to do even the most simple calculations. There are several ways to categorize computers including class, generation and mode of processing.

##### 9.1.1. By Size

Since their introduction in the 1940s, digital computers have evolved into many different types that vary widely in size, cost, computational power, and intended use. Modern computers can be classified as supercomputers, mainframe computers, minicomputers, microcomputers, wearable computers and embedded computers. Supercomputers: A supercomputer is a large, extremely fast, and expensive computer used for complex or sophisticated calculations. Supercomputers are the largest, fastest and most expensive type of computers. They can perform billions of computations per second and are employed for specialized applications that require immense amounts of mathematical calculations. Weather forecasting, scientific simulations, nuclear energy research, and analysis of geological data all require supercomputers. Examples of supercomputers are Fujitsu K computer, IBM Blue Gene, Cray Jaguar and NEC Earth Simulator. Mainframe Computers: A mainframe computer is a high-level, typically large and expensive computer designed to handle intensive computational tasks. Mainframe computers are characterized by their ability to simultaneously support up to hundreds of users connected to the computer by terminals. They operate at very high speed and have very large storage capacities. They are used by large organizations like meteorological surveys and statistical institutes for performing bulk mathematical computations. Minicomputers: A minicomputer is a mid-level computer built to perform complex computations while dealing efficiently with a high level of input and output from users connected via terminals. Minicomputers also frequently connect to other minicomputers on a network and distribute processing among all the attached machines. They are generally used heavily in transaction- processing applications and as interfaces between mainframe computers and wide area networks. Microcomputers: A microcomputer is a computer built around a single-chip microprocessor. Less powerful than minicomputers and mainframes, microcomputers have nevertheless evolved into very powerful machines capable of complex tasks. They are the type of computers that are meant for public use. They are designed to be used by one person at a time. Microcomputers, also called personal computers (PC), can be further classified into desktop computers, laptop computers, Tablet PCs and personal digital assistants (PDAs). Wearable Computers: A wearable computer is a tiny computing device that is worn by the bearer. Wearable computers are also called body-borne computers. These computers are small and lightweight as they need to be carried on human body. Wearable computers come in the form of bracelets, pendent, spectacles and rings. Wearing such devices enable constant interaction with the computing system. Embedded Computers: An embedded computer is a small computer integrated into a larger device or system in order to automatically monitor and control a physical process or environment. They are used for a specific purpose rather than for general processing tasks. Embedded computers control many devices in use today such as mobile phones, microwave ovens, washing machines, vehicles, photocopiers and digital cameras. Users may not even be aware of the role that computers play in such systems.

##### 9.1.2. By Generation

A computer generation is a classification of computers based on changes in technology. Initially, the classification was based on the hardware technology used in building a computer but nowadays, it includes both hardware and software technologies. There are totally five different computer generations, numbered first to fifth. First Generation Major technology

- Vacuum tube Characteristics

- Very large taking up entire rooms

- Consumed a lot of electricity

- Generated a lot of heat

- Used machine language

- Had no operating system

- Performed only one task at a time (single tasking) Vacuum tubes

- Use punched cards and magnetic tapes as input and output media

- Not commercially used Second Generation Major Technology

- Transistor Characteristics

- Smaller, more reliable and energy efficient than 1st generation computers

- Stored their program in an internal memory

- No Operating System

- Single tasking Transistors

- Worked with assembly languages and high-level languages like ALGOL and FORTRAN

- Used punched cards and tapes as input and output media

- Not commercially used Third Generation Major Technology

- Integrated Circuit (IC) Characteristics

- Used keyboards and monitors as input and output media

- Had an Operating System

- Multiprogramming possible

- Flexible and smaller than predecessors

- Well suited for commercial applications Integrated circuit Fourth Generation Major Technology

- Very Large Scale Integration (VLSI) – Microprocessor The microprocessor is a very large scale integrated circuit Characteristics

- Interactive general purpose machines

- Allow rapid application development

- Networking

- GUI and the mouse Fifth Generation Major Technology

- Artificial Intelligence (AI) 5th generation is actually a combination of VLSI and AI. Characteristics

- Portable and sophisticated

- Superior in processing speed

- User friendly

![Applications of Computers](/notes/figures/part2-p028-0.png)

*Applications of Computers*

##### 9.2.1. Commercial and Data Processing Systems

ATM Systems: ATM stands for Automated Teller Machine. It is a machine that is connected to a bank''s computer system that provides the bank''s customers with access to financial transactions in a public space without the need for a cashier. ATMs can be used for a range of banking services like: • Withdrawing cash • Depositing money • Checking the balance of accounts • Transferring money between accounts • Paying bills On most modern ATM systems, a customer is identified by inserting a ATM card with a magnetic stripe or a smart card with a chip, which contains a unique card number and some security information. Authentication is provided by the customer typing a secret PIN (Personal Identification Number) into the ATM''s numeric keypad to confirm that they are the real owner of the card. Upon successful entry of the PIN, the customer may perform a transaction. ATMs can be used by customers of other banks as the ATM can use EFT (Electronic Fund Transfer). If a customer of Bank A uses their debit card to withdraw cash from an ATM belonging to Bank B:

### 1. Bank B gives them the cash

### 2. Bank B now is owed money by Bank A

### 3. Bank B sends an EFT instruction to Bank A asking for money to be

transferred from the customer''s account to Bank B.

### 4. Bank B has now been paid back

ATMs are known by various other names including automated banking machine (ABM), automated transaction machine and cashpoint. Stock Control Systems: The collection of items that a business manufactures or sells is called stock. In a shop for example, the stock includes all of the items on the shelves and out the back in the warehouse. A stock control system is basically a database that keeps track of stock and informs users of when to re-order along with helpful sales reports. When items are sold or delivered, their codes are input to the system either manually or using a point-of-sale terminal (barcode scanner or similar technology). The database matches the codes with the items'' names and prices, prints an itemized bill and uses the data to update stock levels. It also updates a sales file which can be used there and then to calculate all sorts of statistics. Stock control systems make it very easy for stock levels to be monitored, and for stock to be reordered when it is running low. EFTPOS Systems: EFTPOS stands for Electronic Fund Transfer at Point-of- Sale. It is a system that allows individuals to pay for goods using bank cards. To pay for goods, a customer inserts their bank card into a till (cash register) and types in a PIN number to verify that it is their card. The system electronically contacts their bank to check that the card is valid and hasn''t been stolen and also that there is enough fund in their account to pay for the goods. Once payment has been authorized by the bank, money is removed from the customer''s account and electronically paid into the shop''s account.

##### 9.2.2. Modeling and Simulation

Modeling is the process of producing a model; a model is a representation of the construction and working of a real-life system – for example, a spreadsheet (numeric) model of business operations, a computer model of a weather system, a computer model of a car, or a mathematical model of the distribution of matter in the universe. Models are usually produced in order to study the systems they represent. Simulation is the operation or manipulation of a model of a system in order to study the behavior of the real system. Modeling and simulation are useful for studying systems which would be impossible, too expensive to build, impractical or dangerous to work with – for example, designing safer cars, weather forecasting, building better bridges, training pilots, and running a business. Simulation is used before an existing system is altered or a new system built, to reduce the chances of failure to meet specifications, to eliminate unforeseen bottlenecks, to prevent under or over-utilization of resources, and to optimize system performance. Advantages of modeling and simulation:

- It helps reduce the chances of failure to meet specifications

- It prevents over-utilization of resources

- It help avoid danger or loss of life Disadvantages of modeling and simulation

- Simulation process is expensive

- Not all systems can be simulated

- If model is poorly done, results will be faulty

- Simulation results are difficult to translate. It requires an expert Design of Safer Cars: A computer model of a car can be used to test how safe the design of the car is in a crash. The virtual car can be crashed over and over again, the effects investigated and the design easily changed until it is as safe as possible. This is much quicker and cheaper than building and crashing real cars! Weather Forecasting: A computer model of a weather system can be used to predict storms. The wind patterns, temperatures, etc. for the whole planet are simulated using very powerful computers. If the computer model is accurate (it is very difficult to make an accurate model since our planet is rather big) then weather forecasters can use it to ''fastforward'' into the future to see a prediction of what the weather will be tomorrow, next week, next month. Bridge Construction: A computer model of a bridge can be used to test the design. Bridges have to be able to survive extreme weather conditions. It is obvious not practical to build a real bridge and then wait to see if it falls down in a storm. Instead, a computer model of the bridge is created and tested in virtual storms. If the model breaks, it can be quickly and cheaply re-designed and re-tested. If it doesn''t break, the real bridge can be built, confident that it will survive real storms. Bridges can also be tested to see if they can cope with heavy traffic. The virtual bridge can be loaded with a traffic jam of virtual trucks to check that it won''t collapse. A similar system is used by building designers, especially for very large or tall buildings, such as skyscrapers. Running a Business: A spreadsheet model of a business can be used to help predict future profits. If the workings of a business can be modeled accurately, in particular the financial systems, then these models can be used to make predictions. The models are used to help answer ''what-if?'' type questions, e.g. "What if we decrease the workforce by 15%? Will our profits increase or decrease?" Based on the answers that the model gives, the managers of the business can make decisions.

![Trainee  pilots  have  many  hours  of  lessons  in  flight](/notes/figures/part2-p029-0.png)

*Trainee  pilots  have  many  hours  of  lessons  in  flight*

Training of Pilots: Trainee pilots have many hours of lessons in flight simulators before being allowed to fly a real airplane. Flight simulators behave almost exactly like real airplanes since they are controlled by a computer with a very accurate and realistic model of the airplane. The main difference is that the simulator can''t actually crash! Pilots can make mistakes without putting anyone''s life at risk. Flight simulators can provide a pilot with any number of highly realistic flying situations: storms, engine failures, low cloud hiding the runway, etc. The experience that pilots gain whilst using the simulator means that when they eventually start flying real airplanes, they already have many of the required skills.

##### 9.2.3. Monitoring and Control Systems

Computers can be used to monitor and control a system through the use of sensors. Sensors are devices that gather information from the outside world (such as light, temperature, pressure, moisture etc.) and convert it into data that a computer can process. In a monitoring system, the computer analyses the data received and if it is outside normal values, the computer emits and/or gives some form of a reading or printout on a screen, but does nothing to alter the data being read by the system. Monitoring can therefore be defined as the process of using a computer to observe a process and report any deviations. Examples of monitoring are:

- Monitoring of a patient''s vital signs in a hospital

- Burglar or security alarm (where monitoring for intruders is carried out)

- Pollution monitoring in rivers and the atmosphere In a control system, if the received values are outside the acceptable range, the computer sends signals to devices to open/close, switch on or off, etc. so that the computer, in effect, is controlling the process. Consequently, the next sets of sensor readings are different. Control can be defined as the process of using of a computer to observe a process and correct any significant deviations. Examples of control systems are:

- Traffic control (timing of traffic lights)

- Street lighting (controlling the switching on and switching off of street lights)

- Controlling the temperature in a greenhouse Some advantages of using computers to monitor and control processes include:

- It is safer due to faster response to problems in a process

- Computer systems work 24/7 without getting tired. Even though human workforce can operate in shifts key data can be missed during, for example, a shift handover.

- Computers can take and analyze readings more frequently, for example, every 10 seconds which would be difficult for a person to do.

- Computers are generally more accurate so there is less chance of misreading data.

![](/notes/figures/part2-p030-0.png)

##### 9.2.4. Robotics

Robotics is the branch of technology that deals with the design, construction, operation and application of robots. A robot is a machine that is designed to repeatedly do what humans can do with speed and precision. An important application of this technology has been to create robots to perform certain functions that are dangerous for human beings, or to do tasks that can be more effectively performed by machines than by people. Although it may not have the physical appearance of a human being, a robot may be thought of as a machine acting as a person while being controlled by a computer. Some advantages of robots are:

- They rarely make mistakes

- They can produce a greater output in a short amount of time

- They can work at constant speed with no breaks, days off or holidays

- They can work in hazardous conditions such as poor lighting, toxic chemicals or tight spaces

- They are capable of lifting heavy weights without injury or tiring

- They can perform repetitive and boring tasks with speed and continuous precision

- They reduce the amount of wasted material due to their accuracy, thereby reducing costs on waste Some disadvantages of robots are:

- They need power supply to work

- People can lose their jobs

- They need constant maintenance and repairs which cost money

- It costs a lot of money to make or buy a robot

- They are not intelligent. They cannot act differently from what they have been programmed to

- They do not have emotions or conscience which limits how they interact with humans

#### 9.3. Social and Economic Implications of Computers

Computers have had many effects on individuals in society. They have impacted on the way individuals work, socialize and run their lives. As a result of computers, an individual''s values are now in constant flux. The moral and ethical framework that guides an individual is constantly changing as is the economic and legal framework within which lives are led.

##### 9.3.1. Social And Economic Effects

The widespread use of computers has affected people and organizations in many ways. Some of the effects of computers include:

- The introduction of computers have made some jobs outdated leading to unemployment

- New jobs have been created like engineering to build machines, software programming to write programs for the computers, etc

- There is reduction in working hours as a result of automation of some tasks which can lead to reduction of salaries.

- The introduction of computers has led to retraining or reskilling as many employees have to be trained on how to use a computer.

- Replacement of skilled staff by computers leads to deskilling as staff are left to do less skilled jobs. As a result, a skilled employee suddenly is the proud possessor of skills that no-one needs any more.

- Automation of tasks has led to an increase in productivity thereby improving the profit of the organization.

- If staff are striking, work can easily be switched to non-striking staff via a network, even in another country. This is known as electronic scabbing.

- Computers have led to new types of crimes like phishing, identity theft, piracy etc

- The post office has become more of a bill paying center than a communication center as it is now faster and cheaper to communicate online through emails and chats.

- More information can be stored on very little space

- Privacy has become a concern as confidential data stored in computers or distributed across networks can be intercepted by hackers.

##### 9.3.2. Computers in the Workplace

It is important to note that in as much as the computer is a very vital tool for everyone''s daily use, it can also be a very harmful tool if poorly used. Using the computer for prolonged periods of time can lead to physical health risks. For this reason, users must ensure that they follow health measures and apply safety precautions as they use the computers to avoid computer related disorders. Computer Related Disorders: CRDs are health problems associated with prolonged computer usage. They include repetitive strain injury, computer vision syndrome and headache.

- Repetitive Strain Injury (RSI): Body movements are produced by contracting and relaxing muscles. Muscles are attached to bones by tendons. RSI results when repeated stress is placed on the tendons, muscles, or nerves of the body, causing inflammation or damage. RSI is damage to the fingers, wrists and other parts of the body due to repeated movements over a long period of time. RSI is also called, cumulative trauma disorder (CTD), repetitive strain disorder, repetitive stress injury, repetitive stress disorder, overuse syndrome, and musculoskeletal disorder. Some of the most common types of RSI are:

- Tendonitis - inflammation of the tendons

- Tenosynovitis - inflammation of the synovial sheath

- Carpal Tunnel Syndrome - results when the median nerve is compressed, either from the swelling of tendons and sheaths or from repeated bending of the wrist Some of the conditions that may lead to RSI are:

- Repetition - long or concentrated hours of typing or using a mouse

- Posture - long hours of sitting in the same position while typing, especially if it is in an uncomfortable or poorly supported position, or if the wrists are bent

- Lack of rest - intensive hours at the keyboard with no breaks Symptoms of RSI:

- Tingling or numbness in the hands or fingers

- Pain in fingers, hands, wrists, or even shooting up into the arms or forearms

- Loss of strength or coordination in the hands

- Numbness or discomfort in the hands which wakes you up at night

- Computer Vision Syndrome (CVS): CVS or eyestrain refers to problems related to seeing correctly, like visual fatigue, dry itchy and sore eyes, and blurred or double vision, resulting from overuse of a computer monitor. To avoid eyestrain, take the following precautions:

- Exercise your eyes periodically focusing on objects at different distances

- Blink regularly

- Position the monitor to avoid glare

- Keep your monitor clean

- Service, repair or replace a monitor that flickers Ergonomics: It is the science concerned with designing safe and comfortable working environments for humans. This includes furniture design and the design of parts of the computer like the keyboard and mouse. There are a number of practical steps that can be taken to achieve an ergonomically positive environment, and furthermore, to promote a safer working environment. These are:

- Positioning of the person and equipment

- Arranging a safe working environment

- Taking regular breaks

![Body  positioning  and  the  positioning  of  equipment  are](/notes/figures/part2-p031-0.png)

*Body  positioning  and  the  positioning  of  equipment  are*

fundamental to ensuring a comfortable and healthy interaction with computers. A computer user should be able to work in a neutral posture when working with a computer in order to minimize postural demands. A neutral sitting posture is described as:

- Neck is upright and over the shoulders (not bent, twisted, or forward)

- Relaxed shoulders (not slouched or raised)

- Upper arms hanging vertically alongside the body

- Elbows close to the body and at 90 degrees when typing

- Forearms horizontal to the ground

- Wrists straight (not bent up, down or sideways) when typing

- Back straight and well supported by a lumbar support

- Thighs approximately horizontal to the ground

- Feet flat on the floor or a foot rest, with the feet at an angle of 90 degrees with the lower legs The diagrams below highlight some positive and negative body and workstation positioning.

![Workstations  should  be  comfortable  and  have](/notes/figures/part2-p032-0.png)

*Workstations  should  be  comfortable  and  have*

- Workstation layout: Workstations should be comfortable and have sufficient space to allow for freedom of movement. Appropriate placement of workstation components and accessories allows one to work in neutral body position which will enable them perform more efficiently, work more comfortably and safe. As computers generate heat, a well-ventilated room is an important consideration. Coiled cables also give off heat and may need to be rerouted. In addition, securing and covering trailing cables is necessary if hazards are to be avoided.

- Regular Breaks: Computer users in workplaces should take regular breaks if working for protracted periods on a computer. This may mean leaving the workstation for a few minutes every hour to avail of a work- break or to engage briefly in some other work-related activity. Not only will this allow eye muscles to readjust, it will also refresh all of the body''s muscles, promoting personal health and a safe working environment

